    "updated_at": "2023-04-21T17:59:38Z",
    "body": "I'm torn on what the solution should be. The \"proper\" solution is to check, after [step 3.2](https://html.spec.whatwg.org/multipage/popover.html#hide-popover-algorithm) whether *element* is the last item on the popover stack. If it isn't, loop back to 3.1 and hide all popovers until *element* **again**. Repeat until everything is closed up to *element*. That fixes the example above: popover 1 will end up closed.\r\n\r\nOne issue is that this admits an infinite loop, perhaps the one in https://github.com/whatwg/html/issues/9196. But maybe that's just like allowing `while(true) {}` and developers should beware? It is actually an infinite loop to re-open a child popover while trying to close a parent popover.\r\n\r\nThe other alternative is to **stop** after step 3.2 if *element* isn't the last item, but that effectively opens a door to cancelling the `hidePopover()` for a popover, and I think that's actively bad. I'm fairly against this option.\r\n\r\nAny other ideas? Any objections to the \"loop until everything is closed\" approach? I can prototype to make sure it doesn't break anything else, but it shouldn't.\r\n",
    "author_association": "CONTRIBUTOR",
