    "body": "> > Sure, but we already have the \"throw exceptions\" argument in the spec right? I assume in the light dismiss case we'd just return... But yeah I guess then you might want a console warning / error there anyways? But that's more of an implementation detail\r\n> \r\n> Yeah, we could do that. I guess my concern would then be this one:\r\n> \r\n> > The other alternative is to stop after step 3.2 if element isn't the last item, but that effectively opens a door to cancelling the hidePopover() for a popover, and I think that's actively bad. I'm fairly against this option.\r\n> \r\n> ...since an exception here would be the same as a return.\r\n\r\nOkay, I see... wdyt of something like this instead?\r\n\r\n * Hide all popovers (until...) normally.\r\n * If the assertion breaks (and we're not the topmost popover), then hide popovers again, but without firing events this time. Probably add a warning in the console (but that doesn't need to go in the spec).\r\n\r\nThat should:\r\n\r\n * Avoid the subtle infinite loop.\r\n * Avoid the loophole that prevents hiding the popover.\r\n\r\nDoes that seem reasonable?",
