    "body": "Hmm. As I'm implementing this, and looking at tests, I think there's still an infinite loop this doesn't detect. Maybe that's ok. But maybe we actually need to store a nesting level of some kind in order to detect this. Here's the problem case:\r\n\r\n```html\r\n<div id=p16 popover>\r\n    <div id=p17 popover></div>\r\n    <div id=p18 popover></div>\r\n</div>\r\n<script>\r\n  p16.showPopover();\r\n  p18.showPopover();\r\n  p18.addEventListener('beforetoggle', () => p17.showPopover());\r\n  p16.hidePopover();\r\n</script>\r\n```\r\n\r\nIn this example:\r\n1. hidePopover for p16 is called.\r\n2. \"hide all popovers until\" p16, which calls...\r\n3. hidePopover for p18. The beforetoggle handler calls...\r\n4. showPopover for p17. which calls...\r\n5. \"hide all popovers until\" p16, which calls...\r\n6. hidePopover for p18. Loop back to step 3\r\n\r\nTo catch this, you need to store a nesting level somewhere outside the popover, since it's a recursive loop through the event handler. Alternatively, we could store a bit on each popover that gets set when we start trying to do `hidePopover` and gets cleared when we're finished, and it disables the event firing if the bit is already set at the beginning?\r\n\r\nI'm posting this as I encounter it, so let me know if I'm missing something easier.",
